164:/// this run. In production c3 IS the bridge, so this is c3's own pid + start time. Under the
165:/// plugin's PowerShell harnesses c3 runs as a child of a thin shim: the harness monitors and kills
166:/// the SHIM (the process it launched), so the shim exports `CODEX_CONSULT_TEST_BRIDGE_PID` = its
167:/// own pid, c3 records that pid (matching the plugin, where the launched process IS the bridge),
168:/// and [`watch_bridge`] shares the shim's fate. A panel run clears the var when spawning members,
169:/// so each member records its own pid.
170:///
171:/// SECURITY: the hook is honoured only when it names a LIVE ANCESTOR of this process (the shim is
172:/// c3's parent, so it is always an ancestor). An invalid, dead or non-ancestor value is ignored
173:/// silently and c3 falls back to its own pid â€” a stray environment variable can never make c3
174:/// record, monitor or share the fate of an unrelated process. The value is validated exactly once
175:/// at process start and cached.
